      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "To find the smallest positive integer that is both an integer power of 11 and is not a palindrome, we start by considering the first few powers of 11:\n\n\\[\n11^1 = 11 \n\\]\n\n\\[\n11^2 = 121\n\\]\n\n\\[\n11^3 = 1331\n\\]\n\n\\[\n11^4 = 14641\n\\]\n\nWe need to check each of these numbers to see if they are palindromes. A palindrome reads the same forwards and backwards. Let's examine each:\n\n- 11: This is a single-digit number, and it reads the same forwards and backwards. It is a palindrome.\n- 121: This reads the same forwards and backwards. It is a palindrome.\n- 1331: This does not read the same forwards and backwards. It is not a palindrome.\n- 14641: This reads the same forwards and backwards. It is a palindrome.\n\nFrom the above, the smallest positive integer that is both an integer power of 11 and is not a palindrome is \\(1331\\).\n\nThus, the final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{1331}\n\\]",
