    {
      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "To determine the smallest positive integer that is both an integer power of 11 and is not a palindrome, we will follow a systematic approach:\n\nStep 1: Identify the first few positive powers of 11.\nThe first few positive powers of 11 are:\n\\[ 11^1 = 11 \\]\n\\[ 11^2 = 121 \\]\n\\[ 11^3 = 1331 \\]\n\\[ 11^4 = 14641 \\]\n\nStep 2: Check each of these numbers to see if they are palindromes.\nA palindrome is a number that reads the same backward as forward. We will write each power of 11 and check for palindromic properties.\n\n- For \\(11^1 = 11\\), counting from left to right, it reads \\(11\\), which is a palindrome.\n- For \\(11^2 = 121\\), counting from left to right, it reads \\(121\\), which is not a palindrome.\n- For \\(11^3 = 1331\\), counting from left to right, it reads \\(1331\\), which is not a palindrome.\n- For \\(11^4 = 14641\\), counting from left to right, it reads \\(14641\\), which is a palindrome.\n\nStep 3: Note which numbers from this set are palindromes and which are not.\nThe first two numbers, \\(11^2\\) and \\(11^3\\), are not palindromes, while \\(11^4\\) is a palindrome.\n\nSince we are looking for the smallest positive integer that is both a power of 11 and not a palindrome, we select \\(11^2 = 121\\).\n\nThus, the smallest positive integer that is both an integer power of 11 and is not a palindrome is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{121}\n\\]",
